  "prediction": "The report highlights the demographic trends in the technology workforce and the efforts of the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) and the Department of Labor's Office of Federal Contract Compliance Programs (OFCCP) to oversee technology companies and contractors' compliance with equal employment opportunity and affirmative action requirements.\n\nThe technology sector has major employment hubs across the country, including the San Francisco Bay area, the greater New York City region, and the Washington-Arlington-Alexandria region. However, female, Black, and Hispanic workers comprise a smaller proportion of technology workers compared to their representation in the general workforce. From 2005 to 2015, the percentage of women in the technology workforce remained flat, while the percentage of minority workers increased, but with no statistically significant change in the representation of Black workers.\n\nThe EEOC and OFCCP have implemented various measures to address diversity and inclusion in the technology sector. The EEOC has released reports analyzing EEO-1 data on diversity in the technology sector and has initiated an internal working group to identify practices to improve gender and racial diversity in technology. The OFCCP has conducted routine evaluations of technology contractors for compliance with federal nondiscrimination and affirmative action requirements.\n\nHowever, the report identifies several weaknesses in the EEOC and OFCCP's processes that impact the effectiveness of their efforts. The EEOC has not consistently captured industry codes in its investigations, which hinders its ability to conduct industry sector analysis. The OFCCP faces delays in its compliance review process and has not analyzed its closed evaluations to understand the root cause of these delays. Additionally, the OFCCP's current approach to compliance reviews has not changed in over 50 years, and the agency has not evaluated its Functional Affirmative Action Program.\n\nThe report makes six recommendations to the EEOC and OFCCP to improve their oversight of technology companies and contractors. These recommendations include developing a timeline to complete the planned effort to clean IMS data, analyzing internal process data to understand the cause of delays in compliance reviews, and requiring contractors to set placement goals disaggregated by race/ethnicity.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for the EEOC and OFCCP to improve their processes and oversight of technology companies and contractors to address the persistent issues of diversity and inclusion in the technology sector.",
